{# Device dictionary for the 8550-8GB-ssh LAVA device (device-type: ssh) —
   the 8 GB IMDT 8550 SBC, attached via USB/adb to pi-tester-3.

   The board has no network of its own (no cable, no sshd reachable over IP):
   ssh_host/ssh_port are pi-tester-3's OWN sshd, a normal Debian host on the
   lab LAN. The lava-dut key's authorized_keys entry on pi-tester-3 forces
   every session through lava/adb-shell-relay.sh, which transparently relays
   both one-shot commands and the interactive test shell into `adb shell` on
   the DUT attached over USB — see that script for how each case is handled
   (plain exec, the sftp-server subsystem scp defaults to, and the
   root@-prompted interactive shell ssh-tests.yaml's boot action expects).

   ssh_host must NOT be 'localhost': the ssh client runs inside the
   lava-dispatcher container on the compose bridge network, where
   localhost is the container itself.

   The identity file is the private key matching
   recipes-core/lava-ssh-keys/files/lava-dut-key.pub, which must be added to
   pi-tester-3's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys (as the 'pi' user, forced through
   adb-shell-relay.sh — see that script's own header) and installed into the
   dispatcher's worker-state volume:

     docker compose cp lava-dut-key lava-dispatcher:/var/lib/lava/dispatcher/worker/lava-dut-key

   Install on the server with:

     lava-server manage devices add 8550-8GB-ssh --device-type ssh --worker worker0 --tags dut-ssh 8550-8gb
     # then place this file at
     # /etc/lava-server/dispatcher-config/devices/8550-8GB-ssh.jinja2 (owner lavaserver)
#}
{# ssh-keepalive, not stock ssh: adds ServerAlive keepalive to the primary
   connection — see ssh-keepalive.jinja2 for why it has its own name. #}
{% extends 'ssh-keepalive.jinja2' %}
{% set ssh_host = '192.168.50.205' %}
{% set ssh_port = 22 %}
{% set ssh_user = 'pi' %}
{% set ssh_identity_file = '/var/lib/lava/dispatcher/worker/lava-dut-key' %}
